1. VPC Configuration

I created a custom VPC named "Test VPC".

Configuration| Value
VPC name| Test VPC
IPv4 CIDR block| "192.168.0.0/18"
Subnet name| Public subnet
Subnet CIDR block| "192.168.1.0/28"

CIDR note: The original lab scenario references "192.168.1.0/26", while its subnet creation instructions specify "192.168.1.0/28". The configuration above follows the detailed subnet creation instructions.

A subnet provides a defined range of IP addresses within a VPC and allows AWS resources to be organised within the network.

2. Internet Gateway and Route Table

I created an Internet Gateway named "IGW test VPC" and attached it to the custom VPC.

I then created a route table named "Public route table" and configured the internet route.

Destination| Target
"192.168.0.0/18"| Local
"0.0.0.0/0"| Internet Gateway

The "0.0.0.0/0" route directs traffic destined for external networks towards the Internet Gateway.

I associated the public subnet with the public route table to ensure that resources within the subnet could use the configured internet route.

3. Network Access Control List (NACL)

I created a Network ACL named "Public Subnet NACL" and associated it with the VPC's public subnet.

The inbound and outbound rules were configured to allow all traffic.

Direction| Rule| Traffic| Source/Destination
Inbound| 100| All traffic| "0.0.0.0/0"
Outbound| 100| All traffic| "0.0.0.0/0"

This configuration allowed traffic through the subnet-level network control layer during the lab.

NACLs are stateless, meaning inbound and outbound traffic must be evaluated independently.

4. Security Group Configuration

I created a security group named "public security group".

The security group was configured with the following inbound access:

Type| Protocol| Port| Source
SSH| TCP| 22| Anywhere
HTTP| TCP| 80| Anywhere
HTTPS| TCP| 443| Anywhere

Outbound rules allowed all traffic.

Security groups operate at the instance level and are stateful. They control which traffic is permitted to reach an EC2 instance and which outbound traffic can leave it.

5. EC2 Instance Deployment

I launched an Amazon EC2 instance inside the public subnet to test the networking configuration.

Configuration| Value
AMI| Amazon Linux 2023
Instance type| "t3.micro"
Key pair| "vockey"
VPC| Test VPC
Subnet| Public subnet
Public IP assignment| Enabled
Security group| public security group

The instance was successfully launched and reached the Running state.

I used SSH to connect to the instance using the lab-provided credentials.

6. Testing Internet Connectivity

The final objective was to verify that the EC2 instance could communicate with an external destination.

From the EC2 terminal, I executed:

ping google.com

The command returned successful replies from the external destination.

